# The IDE source graph — what monaco-vscode-api gives us, and our ground-up design on it

The demo recipe got the full VS Code workbench rendering in the dash (explorer · editor · panel · terminal ·
status bar, our files in its own explorer). That proved the integration. **This doc throws the copy-pasted look
away** and designs our own IDE deliberately, on top of the API surface we now understand. It is the inventory
(the "source graph") + the from-the-ground-up design. Implementation follows from here, not from the demo.

## A. The source graph — everything we have to work with

### A1. Layout parts (the boxes we arrange)
The workbench exposes **8 parts** (the `Parts` enum); we attach each into our OWN DOM cell (manual-part mode),
so WE own the arrangement — VS Code does not impose its window chrome:

- `TITLEBAR_PART` · `BANNER_PART` · `ACTIVITYBAR_PART` · `SIDEBAR_PART` · `EDITOR_PART` · `PANEL_PART` ·
  `AUXILIARYBAR_PART` · `STATUSBAR_PART`

Today we mount: sidebar · editor · panel · statusbar, with the activity bar relocated to the top
(`workbench.activityBar.location: "top"`). titlebar / banner / auxiliarybar are unused (candidates to stay off
or be repurposed).

### A2. Feature services (the capability surface)
Each capability is a `getXServiceOverride()`. We currently include **34** (the lean set that constructs the
workbench): accessibility · configuration · dialogs · environment · explorer · extensions · files · host ·
keybindings · language-detection · languages · lifecycle · log · markers · model · notifications · output ·
preferences · quickaccess · search · secret-storage · snippets · storage · terminal · textmate · theme ·
user-data-profile · view-banner · view-status-bar · view-title-bar · views · working-copy · workspace-trust.

The full catalogue is ~63; the rest are **opt-in features** we deliberately leave off (debug · scm · testing ·
notebook · chat · ai · tasks · comments · welcome · walkthrough · timeline · outline · speech · survey ·
update · telemetry · mcp · …). **Stripping == this allow-list.** Adding a feature == adding its override.

### A3. Extension / registration points (what we can ADD — the dogfood payoff)
The views override exports real registration APIs: `registerCustomView` · `registerEditor` ·
`registerEditorPane` · `registerEditorSerializer` · `viewContainerRegistry` · `viewRegistry`. So we can put
**our own things inside the workbench**, first-class:

- a **`.work` custom editor pane** — a literate/WYSIWYG editor for workbooks (the `Nexus.Literate` AST), not
  just a text buffer. This is the single most on-brand move available.
- a **Workbooks view container** — our own activity entry with custom views (deploy status, weave output,
  toolkits, code-graph) beside the file explorer.
- custom commands via the command service (`executeCommand`) — we already drive `closePanel`.

Plus **extensions**: Open VSX *web* extensions run client-side (the ext-host worker ships); vsix files load via
the rollup vsix plugin.

### A4. Theming surfaces — and the mechanics finding that decides our approach
There are **four** theming layers; the order matters because we learned the hard way which is robust:

1. **Color theme (registered, generated from our tokens) — THE primary path.** The theme service resolves
   ~600 named colors (`editor.background`, `sideBar.background`, `activityBar.*`, `tab.*`, `statusBar.*`,
   syntax tokens …). We generate a `workbooks-dark` / `workbooks-light` theme from `app.css` and set
   `workbench.colorTheme`. The workbench renders our palette natively. Single source of truth = `app.css`.
2. **`--vscode-*` CSS remap — DEMOTED. Do not rely on it.** *Finding:* in manual-part mode there is **no
   `.monaco-workbench` wrapper element** — the parts are `.part.monaco-workbench-part` attached into our
   cells. All ~291 `.monaco-workbench { --vscode-* }` rules (VS Code's theme AND our remap) therefore match
   nothing; parts fall back to concrete theme values (`rgb(37,37,38)`). That's *why the colors looked wrong* —
   our remap never applied. Use CSS only for layout/spacing/font polish, never as the color mechanism.
3. **File icon theme** — maps file/extension → glyph. We ship one mapping `.work` → our sparks mark and other
   types → iconoir.
4. **Product icon theme** — remaps the entire codicon UI font (every toolbar/view/menu glyph) to our **iconoir**
   set (which the dash already uses). This is how the IDE stops looking like VS Code at the icon level.

Plus **typography** via config (`editor.fontFamily`, `--vscode-font-family`) — Geist / Geist Mono, already wired.

### A5. Control knobs
- **Configuration** (`initUserConfiguration` / settings): `workbench.colorTheme`, `activityBar.location`,
  `panel.defaultLocation`, fonts, minimap, `startupEditor`, etc.
- **Commands** (`executeCommand`): programmatic layout/behaviour (`closePanel`, open view, run task …).
- **Workspace**: a folder URI backed by our in-memory FS (seeded from the mock tree; the real seam is
  WASHIE/`Nexus.Store`).

## B. The ground-up design — our IDE, not VS Code reskinned

Designed on A, deliberately. Each decision names the surface from A it uses.

### B1. Layout — our composition, not VS Code's window
- **NexRail stays the app rail** (Studio · Files · Code · Toolkits · You). The IDE lives entirely inside the
  Code cell. No second left rail — the VS Code activity bar is relocated to the **top** (A1 + A5) or folded
  away; view-switching reads as our own top strip.
- Inside Code: **sidebar** (explorer + our Workbooks views, A3) · **editor** center (with the `.work` custom
  pane, A3) · **panel** (terminal/output) collapsed by default, toggled · a **status strip**. titlebar /
  banner / auxiliarybar: **off** (A1).
- We own the grid (already do), so the proportions, borders (our `--color-line`), and radii are ours.

### B2. Color — generated theme, single source of truth
- A generator reads `app.css` tokens (well/paper/card/ink/dim/line + pastels, both themes) and emits the two
  registered VS Code color themes (A4.1). Editor → `well`, sidebar/activity → `paper`, panels → `card`,
  borders → `line`, accents/focus → `bloom`, syntax → pastels. Switches on the same `data-theme` the dash uses.
- **No `--vscode-*` remap as the color path** (A4.2). CSS is for layout only.

### B3. Icons — iconoir everywhere
- **Product icon theme** (A4.4) remaps the codicon UI font to iconoir — the dash's own icon language — so every
  workbench glyph matches our chrome. **File icon theme** (A4.3): `.work` → sparks, types → iconoir.

### B4. The on-brand build — make it a *workbook* IDE, not a generic one
- Register the **`.work` custom editor pane** (A3) backed by `Nexus.Literate` — literate editing + weave-on-save
  + live preview. This is the dogfood differentiator; a generic Monaco buffer is the fallback, not the goal.
- A **Workbooks view container** (A3): code-graph / deploy / weave-output / toolkits as native views.

### B5. Strip — the allow-list
- Keep the lean 34 (A2). Explicitly **off**: debug · scm · testing · notebook · chat · ai · tasks · welcome ·
  walkthrough · timeline · outline · telemetry · update · survey · speech · mcp. Add back only on demand.

## C. Build order (ground-up)
1. **Color**: the `app.css` → VS Code theme generator + register + set `workbench.colorTheme` (B2). Robust
   theming, replaces the failed CSS-var remap. *Highest-impact: fixes "colors are wrong."*
2. **Icons**: product icon theme + file icon theme → iconoir (B3). *Fixes "icons are wrong."*
3. **Layout polish**: finalize the part grid, borders/radii from tokens, panel toggle UX, kill leftover chrome
   (B1).
4. **On-brand**: the `.work` custom editor pane + the Workbooks view container (B4).
5. **Strip + document** the allow-list (B5); fold findings back here and into [[ide_shell]].

## Build status (ground-up, against C)

- **Step 1 — Color: DONE.** `tools/gen-ide-theme.mjs` reads `app.css` → `theme.generated.js` (146 colors + 16
  syntax rules, dark + light); registered via the theme service; `workbench.colorTheme` set by `data-theme`.
  The whole workbench renders our palette. The `--vscode-*` CSS remap is removed (it could not bind).
- **Step 2 — Icons: DONE.** `tools/gen-ide-icons.mjs` → file-icon theme (`.work`→sparks, types→vscode-icons,
  iconoir folders), registered + set as `workbench.iconTheme`. `tools/gen-ide-product-icons.mjs` → a CSS mask
  layer remapping 48 chrome codicons → iconoir. The explorer + chrome read in our icon language.
- **Step 3 — Layout polish: DONE.** Activity bar relocated to top (no 2nd left rail), panel collapsed (editor
  primary), part grid in our cells, UI font bound to `.monaco-workbench-part` (Geist), dead CSS + dead
  editor-embed `boot.js` removed.
- **Step 5 — Strip / allow-list: IN EFFECT.** The active service set IS the lean **34** (A2). Deliberately
  **OFF** (never added): debug · scm · testing · notebook · chat · ai · tasks · comments · welcome ·
  walkthrough · timeline · outline · speech · survey · update · telemetry · mcp · edit-sessions · emmet ·
  interactive · issue · multi-diff · performance · relauncher · share · assignment · localization ·
  extension-gallery · authentication · remote-agent · treesitter · image-resize · process-explorer. Adding any
  back = adding its override. This is the strip mechanism, documented and in force.
- **Step 4 — On-brand: Workbooks view container DONE; `.work` custom editor pane NEXT.** Via `registerCustomView`
  (A3) a native **Workbooks** view sits beside Explorer/Search (`src/lib/ide/workbooks-view.js`): workspace
  stats, the workspace list, and a working **Weave** action — our content, our palette, rendered inside the
  workbench. Proves we can host Workbooks-specific surfaces in the IDE. The remaining sub-step is the **`.work`
  custom editor pane** (`registerEditorPane` + an `EditorInput` — a literate/WYSIWYG view over `Nexus.Literate`):
  a larger pane implementation, its own focused pass, building on the same registration point now validated.
- **Known non-blocker:** production build (rollup) fails on monaco-vscode worker bundling (bd wb-g4nn). Dev is
  the demo surface; production worker config is a separate task.

## Open questions
- Generated theme: regenerate at build (committed artifact) vs at runtime from live CSS vars? Build-time keeps
  it a reviewable artifact and matches the toolkit-registry pattern.
- Product icon theme authoring: iconoir → a codicon-compatible icon-font/SVG set is real work — do we map the
  ~400 codicons we actually use, or the full set? Start with the visible subset.
- `.work` custom editor: how much of `Nexus.Literate` runs client-side in the demo (no runtime) vs a mock?
- Do we keep VS Code's status bar (themed) or render our own status strip for full control?
